.TH TUI_TERM_WAIT 3 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_term_wait, tui_term_fd \- wait for a key, a resize, or a descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/term.h>
.B #include <poll.h>
.PP
.BI "int tui_term_wait(tui_term *" t ", int " timeout_ms ,
.BI "                  struct pollfd *" extra ", int " n_extra ", tui_event *" ev );
.BI "int tui_term_fd(const tui_term *" t );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR tui_term_wait ()
waits until something happens, at most
.I timeout_ms
milliseconds (negative: no limit; 0: only what is already there), and
describes it in
.IR ev :
.TP
.B TUI_EV_KEY
a key, decoded as
.BR tui_decoder_init (3)
describes.
.TP
.B TUI_EV_PASTE
bracketed paste (with
.BR TUI_TERM_PASTE );
.I ev\->paste
is valid until the next call.
.TP
.B TUI_EV_RESIZE
the terminal size changed, or the session was resumed or continued and
has to be repainted;
.IR ev\->rows " and " ev\->cols
hold the current size.
Several resizes before a call collapse into one.
.TP
.B TUI_EV_FD
an entry of
.I extra
is ready;
.I ev\->fd_index
is its index, and the
.I revents
of the entries are filled in.
The program's own descriptors \(em a network socket, a pipe from a worker
thread \(em are waited on together with the terminal, so the program needs
no second loop.
.TP
.B TUI_EV_HANGUP
the terminal is gone: the input reached its end, or the other side hung
up.
Every later call reports it again at once; the loop must end, since
reading a gone terminal answers immediately and would otherwise spin.
.SS A resize is never lost
The
.B SIGWINCH
handler writes a byte to a pipe that
.BR tui_term_wait ()
polls together with the terminal.
A resize that arrives while the program is drawing, or between any two
calls, leaves that byte behind and wakes the next wait at once \(em there
is no window in which a signal can be latched with nothing left to
interrupt (issue-manager BUG-240), and no polling interval.
.SS ESC and incomplete sequences
A lone ESC is the prefix of every escape sequence.
When the input stops in the middle of one, the wait gives the rest
.I esc_delay_ms
(see
.BR tui_term_open (3))
to arrive; if nothing more comes, a lone ESC is reported as
.BR TUI_KEY_ESC .
The delay keeps running across calls, so a short
.I timeout_ms
does not lose or hurry it.
.PP
Input is read with
.BR read (2),
never through stdio, and
.B EINTR
is retried.
.PP
.BR tui_term_fd ()
returns the input descriptor, for a program that polls by itself and
calls
.BR tui_term_wait ()
with a zero timeout when it is readable.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR tui_term_wait ()
returns 1 with
.I ev
filled, 0 when the timeout passed, and \-1 with
.I errno
on error:
.B EINVAL
for bad arguments,
.B ENOTTY
when a resize was signalled but the size cannot be read, or the error of
.BR poll (2)
or
.BR read (2).
.PP
.BR tui_term_fd ()
returns \-1 for a NULL session.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
for (;;) {
    draw(&model, scr);
    tui_screen_render(scr, &out);
    tui_term_flush_buf(t, &out);

    tui_event ev;
    if (tui_term_wait(t, \-1, NULL, 0, &ev) < 0) break;
    if (ev.type == TUI_EV_HANGUP) break;
    if (ev.type == TUI_EV_RESIZE) tui_screen_resize(scr, ev.rows, ev.cols);
    if (TUI_EV_IS_CHAR(&ev, 'q')) break;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_term_open (3),
.BR tui_decoder_init (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
